---
import type { HTMLAttributes } from 'astro/types';
/**
 * The hero's "how it works" line: a numbered VERTICAL LEDGER under the hero image.
 *
 * Variant 1 of three stacked options, Andrey's pick, 12 Aug 2026. What it replaces: a horizontal
 * rail of numbered discs joined by connector threads, and then, in the same session, three wrong
 * placements of a horizontal spec row. All of it is recorded here because each attempt failed for a
 * measurable reason and the next person should not re-run them.
 *
 * PLACEMENT, three attempts:
 *   1. `grid-column:1/-1` inside `.hero .wrap` — a full-width row across the bottom of the hero. It
 *      stretched to 1144px for four short labels, so each connector ran 217px of empty space.
 *   2. Outside the section entirely, a full-bleed band. That added a fourth horizontal band directly
 *      above the waynav's own rule, and left the gap under the image — the actual problem — empty.
 *   3. Column 2, row 2, UNDER THE IMAGE. Right. The text column runs ~140px taller than the image on
 *      a typical course page and that gap was the one unresolved area of the hero. The placement is
 *      in global.css: the text column spans both grid rows so this sits beneath the image rather
 *      than beneath the whole hero.
 *
 * WHY VERTICAL, and this is the part worth keeping. Horizontal four-across was tried and is not
 * available on this site at any type size worth using. In a 499px column a four-up cell has 107px of
 * usable width. Measured label widths at 12px: "Complete online" 93px, "Sit the assessment" 108px,
 * "Get your White Card" 116px, "Certificate as you finish" 133px, "Get your Statement of Attainment"
 * 193px. Only 8 of 26 pages have four labels short enough to hold one line, and /experts/warwick-smith
 * rendered 1, 4, 2 and 4 lines across its four cells — 46px of raggedness. A content-derived column
 * count (2-up for long labels, 4-up for short) was built and then deleted with this rewrite: stacked,
 * every label on every page gets the full column width and none of them wrap, so the branch had
 * nothing left to decide.
 *
 * The verify-blue last step went with the discs. The hand-off it marked is stated in the step's own
 * words ("Apply to QBCC"), which is where a reader actually reads it.
 *
 * THE EYEBROW is back (12 Aug 2026, Andrey), after being removed earlier the same session to
 * match the approved mock. See the note on `.ht-eyebrow` below for the argument that was overruled.
 *
 * NO BACKGROUND. `.hero` is transparent over the body's --ground, and a filled block under the image
 * would put a third ground inside the hero. The --ink cap rule does the separating.
 *
 * The steps arrive as one " → "-separated string (the frontmatter howItWorks field), so this stays a
 * single source of truth per page and the last step changes with it.
 */
interface Props extends HTMLAttributes<'div'> { steps: string; label?: string; }
const { steps, label = 'Process', ...rest } = Astro.props;

/* HORIZONTAL CARDS, TWO LINES PER STEP (13 Aug 2026, Andrey). This reverses the vertical ledger
   above, and the reversal is only viable because of the two-line split: the measurements that
   killed horizontal last time were all about ONE line not fitting a ~107px cell.

   THE SPLIT IS AUTHORED, NEVER INFERRED, and that is the whole of the design here. A first-word
   split was tested across all 66 steps on all 18 distinct `howItWorks` strings in the repo before
   this was written, and it fails three ways:
     - 11 of 66 steps (17%) are a single word ("Enrol", "Published"), so there is no second line;
     - it splits proper nouns. The step naming the company and an RTO partner would put the
       company's bare three-letter form alone on the first line, which is exactly what the house
       style forbids and what `check-claims` §6 exists to stop. The source string is unchanged, so
       that check CANNOT see it — the breach would exist only in the rendering. The step naming
       Blue Dog Training splits the RTO's name the same way;
       (Written without quoting either offending fragment, because §6 reads component source and
       flagged an earlier draft of this very comment. The check was right: a reader-facing file
       should not carry the bare form even to warn about it.)
     - it is often not action/descriptive at all: "Regulator approves" is noun/verb, "Statement and
       card issued" has no verb in the first position.
   So an author opts in with `|`, and anything without one renders as a single line. A page that has
   not been split yet looks unstyled-but-correct rather than wrong, and no second line is ever
   invented — which is the failure mode recorded against the 12 Aug step-body split, where writing
   the second half of 28 sentences introduced two regulatory claims that were not true. */
const nodes = steps.split(' → ').map((s) => s.trim()).filter(Boolean).map((s) => {
  const at = s.indexOf('|');
  return at === -1
    ? { action: s, detail: '' }
    : { action: s.slice(0, at).trim(), detail: s.slice(at + 1).trim() };
});
---
<div class="howtrack" {...rest}>
  <span class="ht-eyebrow">{label}</span>
  <ol class="ht-steps" role="list" aria-label={label}>
    {nodes.map((n, i) => (
      <li class="ht-cell">
        <span class="ht-n num" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        <span class="ht-t">{n.action}</span>
        {n.detail && <span class="ht-d">{n.detail}</span>}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
</div>
<style>
  /* The cap rule. --ink at 1px, not --rule: at column width this reads as the underline of the image
     above it, which is the relationship wanted — the process belongs to the artefact, not to the
     page. --rule here was too quiet to close anything and the block floated. */
  .howtrack{ border-top:1px solid var(--ink); margin-top:var(--s-md); }

  /* The PROCESS eyebrow, restored 12 Aug 2026 on Andrey's instruction. It has been on, off and on
     again in one session, so the history is worth one line each: the old connector rail carried it;
     the first pass at this rewrite kept it and was told to match the mock, which had none; the mock
     was then overruled and it is back. The argument for removing it — "a repeated tiny tracked label
     over every hero on 26 pages is the scaffolding the brand rules warn about" — is real and is
     recorded as overruled rather than deleted.
     It sits BELOW the --ink cap rule, not above it. The rule is what closes the image; putting text
     between the image and its own underline would break that relationship. Below it, the eyebrow
     labels the ledger it introduces.
     `aria-label`, NOT `aria-labelledby` with an id on this span. That was the first implementation and
     it shipped a duplicate id: `/styleguide` renders this component twice, so one fixed id appeared
     twice on the page, which is invalid and points both lists at whichever node the browser resolves
     first. Caught by counting `id="ht-label"` per built page rather than by looking at one page.
     A per-instance unique id would work but has to come from somewhere — a counter that survives
     Astro's per-render frontmatter, or a random suffix that churns `dist/` on every build. The label
     is one word and identical in both places, so `aria-label` gets the same accessible name with no
     id to collide. */
  .ht-eyebrow{ display:block; margin:14px 0 10px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:11px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:.16em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--slate); }

  /* HORIZONTAL CARDS, 13 Aug 2026, replacing the vertical ladder (whose `--ht-indent` staircase and
     inter-row hairlines are gone with it — there are no rows left to step or to separate).

     `auto-fit` with a 92px floor rather than a fixed `repeat(4,1fr)`. The column this sits in is
     ~499px at desktop, which gives four cards of ~107px; below that the track drops to 2-up and
     then 1-up on its own, which is what makes the same component survive the hero collapsing to a
     single full-width column under 1100px. A fixed four-across is what forced the 12px type floor
     and the 46px of raggedness the previous attempt measured. */
  .ht-steps{ list-style:none; margin:0; padding:0; display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(92px,1fr)); gap:8px; }

  /* Flat, 1px bordered, 6px radius: the content-card shape from DESIGN.md §5, at the smallest size
     the system uses. No shadow, no fill beyond --paper, so it stays inside the hairline vocabulary
     and does not introduce a third ground into the hero (see the NO BACKGROUND note above — the
     cards carry --paper, the block behind them stays transparent). */
  .ht-cell{ display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:2px; padding:10px 12px; border:1px solid var(--rule); border-radius:6px; background:var(--paper); }

  /* The numeral now sits ABOVE the label rather than beside it, so it no longer needs the fixed
     20px gutter that kept a row of labels aligned — a card aligns to its own left edge. `01` not
     `1` still, so the sequence reads as a set rather than depending on the count staying under ten. */
  .ht-n{ display:block; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:10px; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:.08em; color:var(--maroon); }
  /* THE TWO LINES. The action carries the weight and the ink; the detail drops to --slate and a
     step down in size, so a reader scanning four cards reads four verbs first and the qualifiers
     only if they want them. That hierarchy is the reason the split is worth having at all — two
     equal lines would just be a wrapped label.

     13px, half a step under the 13.5px the vertical ledger used. The cells are ~107px at desktop
     rather than the full 499px column, and this is the size at which the longest authored action
     ("Certificate") still holds one line in a 92px card. It stays above the 12px floor the earlier
     horizontal attempts were forced down to, which is what made them unusable.
     Both are under DESIGN.md's 16px body floor because a step label is a label, not prose. */
  .ht-t{ font-family:var(--font-body); font-size:13px; font-weight:600; line-height:1.3; color:var(--ink); }
  .ht-d{ font-family:var(--font-body); font-size:12px; font-weight:400; line-height:1.3; color:var(--slate); }

  /* --slate on --paper is 5.10:1, clear of AA at this size. Do NOT reach for --slate-light here:
     it measures 2.81:1 on --paper and is restricted to inactive-component text and decoration. */

  /* No breakpoint rules. `auto-fit` reflows the track by itself: 4-up in the ~499px desktop column,
     2-up as it narrows, 1-up at the bottom. The @container query and the two breakpoints this
     component once carried are still unnecessary, for a different reason than before. */
</style>
